<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Expats: Ownership Cost Planning for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>The purchase price of a motorbike in Hanoi is the number everyone quotes and the smallest honest part of what the machine will cost, because ownership bills its owner in categories, on schedules the owner does not set, for exactly as long as the machine is owned. This guide writes those categories for the expat planner, refuses to invent a single ownership figure, since used machines, parts and official fees vary too much for honest averages, and contrasts the whole structure with the one set of numbers in this decision that is actually verified and publishable: the rental rates, quoted for comparison only, with the shop that stands behind them named plainly.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk quotes only figures it can stand behind, and sends every ownership number it cannot verify to the offices that own them. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
